          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. How Is Concrete Material Quantity Calculated -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          How Is Concrete Material Quantity Calculated?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Concrete material estimation begins by determining the wet compacted
          volume of the element and converting it to dry loose volume:
        </p>
        <div
          class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-2"
        >
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            Volumetric Estimation Procedure:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
            <li>
              <strong>Wet Volume of Concrete (V<sub>wet</sub>):</strong> Length ×
              Width × Depth × Quantity
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Dry Volume Conversion:</strong> When dry ingredients are mixed
              with water, fine cement and sand particles fill the microscopic voids
              between stone aggregates. A standard <strong
                >Dry Volume Multiplier Factor of 1.54</strong
              > (a 54% volume increase) is applied:
              <br /><code
                >Dry Volume = Wet Volume × 1.54 × (1 + Wastage % / 100)</code
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Proportional Ratio Division:</strong> For a mix ratio of C :
              S : A (Total Parts T = C + S + A):
              <ul class="list-circle pl-5 mt-1 space-y-1">
                <li>
                  <code>Cement Volume = Dry Volume × (C / T)</code> ➔ Weight = Volume
                  × 1,440 kg/m³
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>Sand Volume = Dry Volume × (S / T)</code> ➔ Weight = Volume
                  × 1,600 kg/m³
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>Coarse Aggregate Volume = Dry Volume × (A / T)</code> ➔ Weight
                  = Volume × 1,500 kg/m³
                </li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Understanding Concrete Mix Ratios -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          Understanding Concrete Mix Ratios
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          A concrete mix ratio represents the proportional volume of dry binder,
          fine aggregate, and coarse aggregate. Standard nominal mix ratios
          recognized in civil engineering include:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">
              1 : 1.5 : 3 (M20 Grade)
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Standard mix for reinforced cement concrete (RCC) slabs, roof
              decks, beams, and columns with a target 28-day compressive
              strength of 20 MPa (N/mm²).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">
              1 : 2 : 4 (M15 Grade)
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              General purpose concrete for driveways, footpaths, flooring
              sub-bases, non-structural walls, and light RCC structures (15 MPa
              strength).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">
              1 : 1 : 2 (M25 Grade)
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              High-strength structural concrete for heavy foundations,
              industrial floors, cantilever beams, water tanks, and load-bearing
              columns (25 MPa strength).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
          <em
            >Important Note: The appropriate concrete mix should always be
            selected according to architectural drawings, structural engineering
            designs, and local building codes. Nominal ratios serve as
            estimation guides; commercial infrastructure projects use lab-tested
            design mixes.</em
          >
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. How Many Cement Bags Are Required -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          How Many Cement Bags Are Required for Concrete?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Calculating the exact number of cement bags involves dividing the
          total required cement weight by the packaging size:
        </p>
        <div
          class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-2"
        >
          <p>
            <code
              >Estimated Number of Bags = Total Cement Weight (kg) ÷ Cement Bag
              Size (kg)</code>
          </p>
          <p>
            For example, in a standard 1 m³ pour of M20 (1:1.5:3) concrete with
            5% wastage:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
            <li>Dry Volume = 1.0 m³ × 1.54 × 1.05 = 1.617 m³</li>
            <li>Total Mix Parts = 1 + 1.5 + 3 = 5.5</li>
            <li>Cement Volume = 1.617 × (1 / 5.5) = 0.294 m³</li>
            <li>Cement Weight = 0.294 m³ × 1,440 kg/m³ = 423.36 kg</li>
            <li>
              <strong>50kg Bags Required:</strong> 423.36 ÷ 50 ≈ <strong
                >8.5 Bags</strong>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Concrete Material Calculation Example -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          Concrete Material Calculation Example
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Consider casting an RCC floor slab with dimensions of <strong
            >10.0m Length × 5.0m Width × 0.15m Thickness (150 mm)</strong
          > using an <strong>M20 (1:1.5:3)</strong> mix, a <strong
            >1.54 dry factor</strong
          >, and <strong>5% wastage</strong>:
        </p>
        <div
          class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1.5 font-mono"
        >
          <div>
            • Wet Concrete Volume = 10.0m × 5.0m × 0.15m = <strong
              >7.500 m³ (264.86 CFT)</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            • Base Dry Volume = 7.500 m³ × 1.54 = <strong>11.550 m³</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            • Dry Volume with 5% Wastage = 11.550 × 1.05 = <strong
              >12.128 m³ (428.28 CFT)</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            • Total Ratio Parts (1 + 1.5 + 3) = <strong>5.5 Parts</strong>
          </div>
          <div class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-bold">
            • Cement Required: 12.128 × (1 / 5.5) = 2.205 m³ ➔ 3,175.2 kg ➔ <strong
              >63.5 Bags (50kg)</strong>
          </div>
          <div class="text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-bold">
            • Sand (Fine Aggregate): 12.128 × (1.5 / 5.5) = 3.308 m³ ➔ <strong
              >116.8 CFT (5.29 Tonnes)</strong>
          </div>
          <div class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 font-bold">
            • Coarse Aggregate: 12.128 × (3 / 5.5) = 6.615 m³ ➔ <strong
              >233.6 CFT (9.92 Tonnes)</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            • Estimated Water (0.50 W/C): 3,175.2 kg × 0.50 = <strong
              >1,588 Liters</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 italic">
          * This example is for material estimation only. Actual quantities may
          vary based on mix design, site compaction, and aggregate moisture.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. Common Mistakes -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          Common Mistakes When Estimating Concrete Materials
        </h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Using Wet Volume Directly
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Dividing wet concrete volume directly among mix ratios without
              applying the 1.54 dry volume multiplier leads to a 35%
              underestimation of cement, sand, and aggregate.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Ignoring Wastage
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Handling losses, shuttering bulge, transit spillage, and uneven
              foundation excavation require a 5% to 10% allowance to avoid site
              shortages.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Using the Wrong Mix Ratio
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Applying a lean 1:3:6 PCC mix for structural RCC columns or slabs
              compromises load-bearing capacity and structural safety.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Ignoring Moisture Content (Bulking of Sand)
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Damp sand increases in volume by up to 25% due to moisture
              bulking, requiring on-site batching volume corrections.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Assuming Cement Bag Sizes Are Constant
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Packaging sizes vary globally (50 kg in India/UK, 94 lbs in the
              US, 40 kg or 25 kg in specialized retail). Always verify the bag
              weight.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Treating an Estimate as a Final Mix Design
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Structural concrete for commercial and multi-story construction
              must be verified with formal laboratory concrete mix designs (IS
              10262 / ACI 211).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 7. Important Information & Disclaimer -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          Important Information and Disclaimer
        </h2>
        <div
          class="rounded-xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50/60 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-900/40 dark:bg-amber-950/30 dark:text-amber-300 space-y-2"
        >
          <p>
            • All numerical outputs provided by this calculator are engineering
            estimates based on entered dimensions, standardized density
            assumptions, and nominal ratios.
          </p>
          <p>
            • Dry volume factors (typically 1.54) account for interstitial void
            displacement but may vary slightly depending on coarse aggregate
            gradation and sand fineness modulus.
          </p>
          <p>
            • Water requirements depend on aggregate surface moisture, ambient
            temperature, desired slump/workability, and plasticizer admixtures.
          </p>
          <p>
            • Concrete for critical load-bearing structural work (columns,
            foundations, transfer girders) must follow approved engineering
            drawings and certified mix designs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
